WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.940
In esports there are stories of prodigies.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.680
(Casting)

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.060
Heroes who have triumphed thanks to their
undying love of the game.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.780
And players that proved their doubters wrong
through hard work and skill alone.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.340
(Casting)

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.680
While all have faced adversity in their quests
for greatness, none have earned the title

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.930
of “the greatest” and willingly chosen
to walk away from it.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.440
None, except for ZeRo.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.220
(Casting)

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.520
But the story of the King of Smash 4 isn’t
made of happy memories.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:43.980
ZeRo’s throne is a lonely one, sitting in
a kingdom built on a foundation of torment,

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.820
years of hard work, and a role he never wanted:
the villain of someone else’s story.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.120
"Even people that I will consider my friends actually want me to lose"

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:59.060
ZeRo’s reign was incredible, but the path
he took to get to the top was greater still.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.160
This is his story.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.080
Gonzalo “ZeRo” Barrios was born on April
17, 1995 in Chillán, Chile.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.180
He started playing Smash in 1999, at the age
of four, when the original Super Smash Bros.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.020
released on the Nintendo 64.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.500
But ZeRo had a difficult childhood.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:23.020
"I pretty much from the very beginning I saw the ugly side of many things."

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:28.100
"A few things happened, you know, very very awful experiences happened where kind of like everything shattered."

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.880
"So then my parents were not together anymore."

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:38.000
"The sense of family was destroyed. I didn’t have a stable home for like the majority of my childhood."

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.760
"I was pretty much in poverty for a a long period of time."

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.960
After his parents’ divorce, his mother
was unable to find steady work.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.590
And while his family’s financial struggles
may have taken a toll on him, the bullying

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.000
he experienced at school was worse still.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.040
"In school I was always the fat kid that no one really gave a shit about."

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:01.420
"No one cared. Like whatever I thought about anything, they never cared."

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.220
"I was always quiet because people always told me to shut up."

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:09.400
Zero ended up dropping out of school, but
he found refuge in a Gamecube his mother bought

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.080
him.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:15.111
After spending countless hours in front of
a TV playing Super Smash Brothers Melee, ZeRo

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.440
decided it was time to test his skill against
others.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:23.200
ZeRo attended his first Melee tournament in
2006, but didn’t actually play in it.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:34.540
"I just couldn't. I just couldn't play. I just turned around and told my mom 'let's go home, I'm too nervous."

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.000
Eventually, ZeRo would not only overcome his
fear of competition, but grow to love the

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.200
thrill of it.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.030
From there ZeRo was hooked.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:46.300
He started going to more tournaments, and
when he wasn’t competing he worked , picking

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.319
blueberries with his mother so that he could
afford to go to even more Smash events.

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:53.060
He began dominating the local Chilean scene.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.600
First in Melee, then in its sequel, Super
Smash Bros. Brawl.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:01.340
He discovered that, in America, people
like Melee legend Ken “Ken” Hoang and

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:06.920
Halo champion Tom “TSquared” Taylor were
making a living as professional video game players.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:12.240
In 2011, ZeRo picked up his first recorded
tournament victory: first place in Chile’s

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.340
second national Brawl tournament.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.350
After repeated successes on home soil, ZeRo
realized that it was time to take his talents

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:19.350
to America.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:24.220
ZeRo’s first ever international event
was Apex 2012, where he placed 17th in singles

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.400
and ninth in doubles.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.700
(Casting)

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.280
But placing that low wasn’t enough to make living on.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.800
ZeRo returned to Chile, but the promise of
being able to live off Smash like others were

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:37.310
doing was intoxicating.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:42.400
In 2013, ZeRo asked his mother to let him
go to the United States for one year to try

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.600
and make a career out of Smash.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.790
If he couldn’t do it, he’d go back home,
maybe go to college, and leave the game behind

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:48.790
for good.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.330
ZeRo moved to Philadelphia and began to eke
out a living.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:55.480
He would practice Brawl, compete in tournaments,
and use the money he earned to pay his friend’s

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:58.200
family some rent for letting him stay in their basement.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:06.040
“In fact, they didn't even charge me anything for the first few months because they wanted to me to get a foot on the ground pretty much, and kind of like start making some money."

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:13.020
"And then they charged me a little bit. They pretty much charged me like $200 a month just to live there.”

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.540
Which was extremely generous.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.100
Everything else was re-invested into Smash
so that he could attend more tournaments.

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:25.319
He spent hours on buses, going from Philadelphia
to New York to New Jersey to California to

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:30.200
Maryland, all while studying videos of
top Brawl players to find weaknesses in their game.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.740
And his studies paid off.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:35.400
ZeRo turned heads with wins over some of Brawl’s
best players.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:43.500
(Casting)

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.819
In private, Mew2King told him he had what
it took to become one of the best.

00:04:47.819 --> 00:04:52.229
Over the next while, ZeRo competed and placed
at a myriad of tournaments, and in time, his

00:04:52.229 --> 00:04:54.389
wins began outpacing his rent.

00:04:54.389 --> 00:04:58.669
However, despite being successful enough to
no longer worry about making a living, ZeRo

00:04:58.669 --> 00:05:03.340
never established himself as a dominant force
in Brawl on the level that Mew2King and Nairo were.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.000
But then, he got his big break.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:11.100
At E3 2013, Nintendo unveiled Super Smash Brothers for 3DS and Wii U.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:17.420
With series creator Masahiro Sakurai saying that the game would fall somewhere between the pace of Melee and Brawl.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:23.340
For E3 2014, just a few months ahead of the game's launch, Nintendo held an invitational tournament

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.700
with some of Smash’s biggest
names, including ZeRo.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.300
With the world watching, ZeRo didn’t waste
his opportunity.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:43.760
(Casting)

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:48.820
"The body high that you get off of a win in those conditions is inexplicable."

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:57.400
"Like it is the kind of body high that I can be 80 years old and I will still remember that, because it was that impactful."

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.400
"I will say it was one of the best moments I have ever lived in my existence."

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:10.120
Sure, the event didn’t exactly play by official tournament rules, but his grand finals win over Hungrybox put ZeRo on the map.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.420
ZeRo kept playing Melee and Brawl up to Smash
4’s release, but Smash 4 called to him.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:19.499
At Sky’s Smash 4 Invitational, the first
tournament to be played with competitive rules,

00:06:19.499 --> 00:06:20.830
ZeRo placed third.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:21.830
After that?

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:22.990
There was no stopping him.

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:27.819
First at UGC Biweekly, Triumph or Die 2014,
Super Smash Weekend, Super Smash Saturdays,

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:32.090
UGC Biweekly, Double Vegas Down Attack , SkillCon,
USC Smash, Warriors Path, CLASH Online Invitational,

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:36.619
Double Vegas Down Attack, Final Battle, Smash
4 Worldwide, COTU 6, Apex 2015, Showdown 6,

00:06:36.619 --> 00:06:40.009
Cyberzone, The Gaming Zone, Shockwave, Aftershock,
Double Vegas Down Attack, Come on and Ban,

00:06:40.009 --> 00:06:44.080
NorCal Regionals 2015, MVG Sandstorm, Chokaigi
2015, Fire and Dice, Smash 4 Oomba , Oblivion

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.470
Tournaments Bi-weekly , Smash 4 Oomba, Fire
and Dice, Double Vegas Down Attack, Fire and

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:50.729
Dice, Fire and Dice, BESST 2015, Combo Breaker
2015, Double Vegas Down Attack 10, La Paradiso,

00:06:50.729 --> 00:06:55.809
CEO 2015, FC Smash 15XR, EVO 2015, MSS 1,
Top Cut Comics and Poplar.

00:06:55.809 --> 00:06:59.419
43 tournaments into the longest win
streak in esports history, ZeRo was signed

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:00.419
by Team SoloMid.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:02.210
And he wasn’t done yet.

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:08.690
Between November 2014 and October 2015, ZeRo
won an unprecedented 53 tournaments in a row.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:11.210
53 tournaments in 11 months.

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:15.020
ZeRo wasn’t just winning a few big tournaments
and ignoring the rest, he was grinding out

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:18.240
more than a tournament a week all over the
United States.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.229
If you had a local, ZeRo was willing to make
the trip and take your money too.

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:24.100
And he wasn’t just winning.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.460
He was dominating.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:30.960
(Casting)

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.680
He wasn’t just the best. He was unbelievable.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:42.120
(Casting)

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:47.580
Perhaps his most impressive individual feat
came at EVO 2015, when ZeRo became the first

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:51.500
Smash player to ever win the tournament without
dropping a single game.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:08:01.340
(Casting)

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.979
Back in his glory days, Ken, the man ZeRo

00:08:03.979 --> 00:08:08.280
looked up to at the beginning of his career,
was known as the King of Smash – ZeRo became

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.259
the first person to ever truly inherit that
title.

00:08:11.259 --> 00:08:17.580
But when you’re the king, you will always
face a challenge to your crown, and at MLG

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:21.760
New Orleans, Nairo would bring ZeRo’s incredible
streak to a dramatic end.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:44.720
(Casting)

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:49.959
Despite losing his air of invincibility, ZeRo
bounced back, proving why he had reigned for

00:08:49.959 --> 00:08:51.000
so for so long.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:09:01.420
(Casting)

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:07.280
But after 64 recorded first place finishes
in just over a year, an injury forced him

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:08.300
onto the sidelines.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:13.980
"So every time I shield or tech it really hurts. So imagine every time you're playing the game, some actions will pain you."

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:18.840
"It's not the type of pain where you get pinched or something, it's the type of pain where like someone hits you."

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:20.120
"It pains really bad."

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.660
Even during his hiatus, ZeRo was ranked as
the greatest Smash 4 player in the world.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:28.899
But two months was a long time to go without
real practice and it started to look like

00:09:28.899 --> 00:09:31.579
his opposition was beginning to catch-up.

00:09:31.579 --> 00:09:36.269
ZeRo finally returned to competition in Canada
at Get On My Level 2016, where he managed

00:09:36.269 --> 00:09:40.100
to place second. It was an incredible showing for someone who hadn’t played seriously in

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:41.100
two months.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.440
But ZeRo’s results were going to remain
inconsistent.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.720
Second at 2GGT: Mexico Saga, second at Smash
‘N’ Splash 2,

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:54.300
Ninth at CEO, his first time missing Top 8
in two years.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:10:01.340
(Casting)

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:10.380
Third at WTFox 2, third at EVO 2016, and ZeRo
hit his lowest point yet with 13th at Super

00:10:10.399 --> 00:10:12.000
Smash Con 2016.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:20.840
(Casting)

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:25.700
ZeRo was beating solid players, but losing
to lower ranked, or at the time unknowns.

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:28.100
People were ready to write him off.

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:33.481
But he triumphed at Endgame in Illinois, beating
EVO champion Ally and crushing Mew2King with

00:10:33.481 --> 00:10:36.300
a perfect JV3 stock in the Grand Finals.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:40.620
(Casting)

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:46.079
ZeRo won Shine 2016 the next week without
dropping a single set, a performance reminiscent

00:10:46.079 --> 00:10:47.540
of his dominant era.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:49.540
But again, his form dipped.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:55.750
ZeRo took a fall in March 2017, finishing
49th at 2GGC: Civil War, his lowest placing

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:56.250
ever.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:11:04.580
(Casting)

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:09.820
After that, he only missed Top 8 twice, and
brought home 31 first place trophies.

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:16.280
Including wins at CEO Dreamland, 2GGC: Nairo
Saga, CEO and Shine 2017.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:17.940
(Casting)

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.891
ZeRo achieved everything he wanted to do when
he left Chile.

00:11:20.891 --> 00:11:25.459
He was the absolute best player Smash 4 had
ever seen.

00:11:25.459 --> 00:11:29.000
There was no one left to beat, nothing left
to conquer.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:33.680
"I wanted to beat everybody, I wanted to beat every single character, every single player."

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:40.960
"I wanted to beat the best players from Japan, the best players from every state. I just wanted to build something that I felt was unshakeable."

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:50.480
"That I felt I would be so satisfied with that I could just, turn the page on it and look back on it and say I did all that work for that. I built something that I'm proud of."

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:54.520
The same day he announced his retirement,
ZeRo was ranked first again.

00:11:54.529 --> 00:11:59.050
Even at the very end, he was still the best player
in the world.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:03.000
From the height of his dominant streak to
inconsistent days of placing outside of Top

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:08.269
8, ZeRo earned every win, every triumph, and
every bit of praise he received.

00:12:08.269 --> 00:12:14.019
ZeRo started from nothing, but by dedicating
himself to Smash, through making it his life.

00:12:14.019 --> 00:12:15.100
He found success.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:22.120
“You know, even if I have the worst loss of all time, even if I feel like the game sucks – whatever reason I feel at the moment –

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:26.500
"I still always come back to it, I still always enjoy it. It still always makes me feel the most out of anything."

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:31.560
ZeRo went from no one to one of esports’
best. From a poor kid in Chile to the King

00:12:31.569 --> 00:12:32.579
of Smash 4.

00:12:32.579 --> 00:12:37.880
He scrounged by on tournament winnings, never
underestimated his opponents and always studied,

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:39.200
always practiced for the next fight.

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:43.060
"My goal was never to like make money with this, or be popular."

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:56.400
"I just hope that other people that may be in my position just look at it, and says 'hey, if this person so few resources could manage to do all this, get this far, maybe I can do it too with anything.'"

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:02.120
Regardless of whether ZeRo returns one day
to dominate as he did over the last few years,

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:06.639
nothing can take away from his legacy and
the obstacles he overcame along the way to

00:13:06.639 --> 00:13:08.000
attain it.

